# FigDown — figures as text. Spec: https://github.com/FigDown/figdown

figdown 0.1 topology
# ARP address resolution (RFC 826): Host A needs the MAC for B's IP.
# A broadcasts "who-has", B replies unicast, A caches the mapping.
# The broadcast vs unicast distinction is a CLASS (meaning in the label,
# dashed is only its presentation, `PRESENTATION-AS-MEANING-CARRIER`) — presentation is never the sole
# carrier. "The rest of the LAN" is an external, not an invented node.
#
# HONEST LIMIT (guide/expressing.md "strict message ordering"): step order rides on
# the 1/2 label ordinals, a naming convention; the sequence genre
# (`MESSAGE-ORDER-AND-STATE`, closed 0.4) makes it first-class, and this figure stays
# figdown 0.1 deliberately for portability. The cache update is a local
# action on A, not a message — it rides on the reply label and the prose
# (mode 3: figure + prose are one deliverable), not on a self-edge.
title "ARP Resolution"

# Semantic classes: delivery scope. Label = MEANING; dashed = presentation
# only; the legend derives automatically (spec §2.7, `PRESENTATION-AS-MEANING-CARRIER`).
class bcast "Broadcast — delivered to every host on the LAN (dst MAC ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff)" stroke=#dc2626 style=dashed
class ucast "Unicast — delivered only to the requester (dst MAC = A's MAC)" stroke=#16a34a

node a "Host A\nwants MAC for B's IP"
node b "Host B\nowns the target IP"

# Every other station on the segment hears the broadcast but is outside the
# resolution proper: an external, drawn as an open edge end (spec §2.8).
external lan "rest of the LAN\n(hosts C, D, ...)"

flow right

# Step 1: A broadcasts the request to the whole segment. The same broadcast
# reaches every other station too; they ignore it (short label — detail here
# and in the showcase prose).
edge a -[1: who-has B?  (broadcast)]-> b class=bcast
edge a -[1: heard, ignored]-> lan class=bcast

# Step 2: B replies to A directly (unicast); on receipt A caches the
# IP -> MAC binding — a local action on A, stated on the label, not a message.
edge b -[2: B is at aa:bb:cc:...  A caches it]-> a class=ucast

# Hybrid panels: a second figdown section below (multi-section `MULTI-FIGURE-DOCUMENTS`),
# not nested under the scene header. Each section has one genre (`GENRE-KEYWORD-ALLOWLIST`).

# --- panel: separate genre section (main-standard hybrid = multi-section `MULTI-FIGURE-DOCUMENTS`) ---
figdown 0.1 table
table cache "Host A's ARP cache — before and after the exchange"
| Moment             | Entry                                   |
|--------------------|-----------------------------------------|
| before the exchange| (no entry for B's IP)                   |
| after segment 2    | B's IP → aa:bb:cc:... , learned from the reply |
